<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Teesta River</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tista River</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Teesta is a river that rises in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalayas</a> of Sikkim and flows south through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bengal">West Bengal</a> into Bangladesh, where it joins the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmaputra">Brahmaputra</a> (Jamuna). About 414 km long, it marked the eastern limit of Gorkha expansion in the late 18th century and the western edge of the Koch kingdom of Cooch Behar. Sharing its waters remains a long-running issue between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and Bangladesh.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/teesta-river.jpg" alt="Teesta River" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Indrajit Das · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Teesta_River_View_From_Sikkim.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
